# ModemManager -- the daemon behind gnome-control-center's Mobile Broadband
# panel, and the source of the mm-glib that NetworkManager needs.
#
# TWO CONSUMERS, AND THE SECOND IS WHY THIS IS SEQUENCED WHERE IT IS.
# NetworkManager's meson.build 610-611 makes mm-glib a BARE dependency() with
# no required:false whenever -Dmodem_manager is on, so NetworkManager must be
# built AFTER this package or built without modem support and rebuilt later --
# and a rebuild of a published package costs a subversion bump. The agreed
# order is libgudev -> ModemManager -> NetworkManager.
#
# BLFS 12.4. NOT A RELEASE TARBALL: upstream publishes ModemManager through
# GitLab's archive endpoint, so the URL is a /-/archive/ path rather than a
# /-/releases/ one, and it is a .tar.gz rather than the .tar.xz every other
# recipe here fetches. Both are the book's, and the md5 below matches the one
# BLFS 12.4 publishes (b72860c54670f362e947edba160fa98e) -- checked against the
# book rather than only against the bytes that arrived, which is the difference
# between "what I downloaded" and "what upstream released".
#
# A git archive normally raises the question of whether the tree is missing
# generated files. Here it is not: meson.build line 6 carries version '1.24.2'
# literally, so nothing is derived from .git.
SRC_URL=https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mobile-broadband/ModemManager/-/archive/1.24.2/ModemManager-1.24.2.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=fbc75adcc0d7b0565f256e7ff4e8872b0a37c4413ff576665f7470932d9c1b68
SRC_DIR=ModemManager-1.24.2

# -Dpolkit=strict. THE SAME DECISION NetworkManager's -Dpolkit MAKES, and it
# fails the same way if it is got wrong. meson.build 238-245:
#
#     enable_polkit = (polkit_opt != 'no')
#     policy_conf = {'MM_DEFAULT_USER_POLICY':
#                    (polkit_opt == 'permissive' ? 'yes' : 'auth_self_keep')}
#
# so 'permissive' compiles in a default policy of "yes" -- every caller
# allowed, no authentication -- and 'no' removes the authorization layer
# altogether. 'strict' is the default and is written down anyway, because the
# other two are one word away and neither announces itself at runtime.
#
# -Dmbim=false -Dqmi=false -Dqrtr=false. THE LARGEST CAPABILITY LOSS IN THIS
# RECIPE AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL TODAY. All three default TRUE, and each is a
# bare dependency() that hard-errors when its option is on (meson.build 261,
# 268, 278): mbim-glib >= 1.32, qmi-glib >= 1.36, qrtr-glib >= 1.0. None of
# libmbim, libqmi or libqrtr-glib is packaged.
#   WHAT IS LOST: MBIM and QMI are the two control protocols essentially every
#   modern LTE and 5G modem speaks. What remains is the AT-command path, which
#   covers older and simpler devices. So this is not "mobile broadband works
#   slightly less well" -- it is "most current modems will not be driven at
#   all", while the panel, the daemon and the D-Bus API are all present and
#   correct. Recorded here and printed by post-install.sh on every build,
#   because nothing in a build log or an installed tree shows it.
#   qrtr additionally asserts qmi is enabled (line 277), so the three are not
#   independent: qmi is the one to package first if this is ever revisited.
#
# -Dsystemdsystemunitdir=no. THE SAME STRING-OPTION-AS-AUTO-FEATURE TRAP AS
# NetworkManager, and finding it twice in one chain is the argument for
# treating it as a class rather than a quirk. meson.build 205-210: the option
# is type string with default '', and '' is treated as "go and find systemd"
# rather than as "no" -- it reaches for systemd_dep, which elogind does not
# provide (elogind ships libelogind.pc and a libsystemd.pc symlink, not
# systemd.pc). An empty default that means auto-detect is invisible in
# meson_options.txt, where it reads as an unset path.
#
# -Dsystemd_suspend_resume=false and -Dsystemd_journal=false. Both default
# TRUE. There is no systemd and no journal here; elogind covers the login side
# and nothing covers the journal.
#
# -Dudevdir=/usr/lib/udev and -Ddbus_policy_dir=/usr/share/dbus-1/system.d are
# both named rather than left to their empty defaults, for the reason bluez and
# fuse3 name theirs: the defaults are derived from pkg-config, /lib is a
# symlink duct-filesystem owns, and a derivation that moves would move these
# with it. /usr/share rather than /etc for the D-Bus policy matches bluez,
# wpa_supplicant and NetworkManager -- /etc is for administrative overrides.
#
# -Dbash_completion=false. DEFAULTS TRUE AND STOPPED THE BUILD, which is how it
# was found rather than by reading: meson.build 181 is a bare
# dependency('bash-completion') with no required:false, and bash-completion is
# not packaged. It is worth recording HOW it was missed -- the option exists,
# but meson_options.txt puts it after forty-odd plugin_* entries, so a first
# reading that stopped at the interesting options never reached it. Listing the
# non-plugin options explicitly is what found it.
#   What is lost is tab completion for mmcli in bash. Nothing else: the
#   dependency exists only to locate a completions directory.
#
# -Dman=true is the default and is KEPT, unusually for this tree, because it
# costs nothing: docs/man/meson.build is a plain install_man of two
# pre-written pages, with no help2man or docbook step behind it. Checked rather
# than assumed, since a man option is normally a tooling dependency.
#
# -Dexamples=false and -Dtests=false, both defaulting TRUE and neither built by
# anything here. -Dat_command_via_dbus stays false, its default: it exposes a
# D-Bus method for sending arbitrary AT commands to a modem, which is a
# debugging facility and an attack surface.
#
# -Ddbus_policy_dir is pinned rather than left empty for a sharper reason than
# the usual one: meson.build 175-177 derives the default from dbus's
# SYSCONFDIR, not its datadir, so the unpinned answer is /etc/dbus-1/system.d.
# That is the administrator's directory. bluez, wpa_supplicant and
# NetworkManager all put their own policy under /usr/share, and a package's own
# file belongs where the package manager owns it.
#
# The plugin_* options are left alone. They are `auto` features for individual
# modem vendors that build from source in this tree with no external
# dependency, so `auto` resolves them all on -- which is what is wanted, and
# unusually for an auto feature costs nothing.
MESON_ARGS="-Dpolkit=strict \
-Dmbim=false -Dqmi=false -Dqrtr=false \
-Dsystemdsystemunitdir=no -Dsystemd_suspend_resume=false -Dsystemd_journal=false \
-Dudevdir=/usr/lib/udev -Ddbus_policy_dir=/usr/share/dbus-1/system.d \
-Dexamples=false -Dtests=false -Dbash_completion=false \
-Dintrospection=true -Dvapi=false"
